<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
          xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">
        <!-- screenOrientation is locked to portrait. The game is a fixed
             portrait canvas; landscape would letterbox it into a stripe down
             the middle of the screen. Locking it here rather than hoping is
             what stops a child rotating the phone mid-run.

             windowSoftInputMode is adjustNothing: when the keyboard opens,
             do not resize the window and do not pan it. Without this Android
             shrinks the web view, Phaser sees the window change size and
             rescales the whole canvas, and the name box is dragged away from
             the card it belongs to.

             The Capacitor keyboard setting alone did not achieve this - the
             activity's own soft-input mode was still winning, which is why
             the first attempt at this made matters worse rather than better.
             The keyboard now draws on top, and the finish card lifts itself
             clear in code. -->
        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:screenOrientation="portrait"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustNothing"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS" android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>

    </application>

    <!-- Permissions: none, deliberately.

         INTERNET was here only so the advert banner could be fetched. With
         the adverts gone nothing in this app ever reaches the network: the
         game, the Phaser engine, every sound and every piece of artwork are
         bundled inside the package, and the high scores never leave the
         device.

         Deleting the line was not enough. Capacitor's own library declares
         INTERNET in its manifest, and the build merges every library's
         declarations into the app's - so it came straight back. The
         directive below is the override that removes it from the merged
         result. Verified by reading the merged manifest, not by assuming.

         Declaring no permissions is worth more than it looks on a
         children's app. Play shows a parent exactly this list, and the
         claim cannot be fudged: the system enforces it whatever the code
         tries to do.

         The build now prints "was tagged to remove other declarations but
         no other declaration present". That warning is expected and is not
         a problem: the AdMob plugin was what re-added INTERNET, so with it
         uninstalled there is nothing left to remove. The directive stays
         anyway, as a guard. A permission appearing because some library
         decided to declare one is exactly how AD_ID arrived here unasked.

         TO PUT IT BACK, if the game ever needs the network: delete the
         tools:node attribute below, leaving the plain uses-permission line.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" tools:node="remove" />
</manifest>
